-- title:
--   Arrival index of the Kaplan lead-time model, $\lambda_n = \max(\lambda_{n-1}, n - \rho_n)$
-- statement:
--   In the Kaplan lead-time model (Assumption 2 of the paper), the arrival index is generated by a sequence $\rho = (\rho_n)_{n \ge 0}$ of nonnegative integers (a realization of i.i.d. random variables). The orders received in period $n$ are those outstanding at the start of period $n$ that were placed in period $n - \rho_n$ or earlier. The arrival index is defined recursively by
--
--   $$
--   \lambda_0 = 0, \qquad \lambda_n = \max(\lambda_{n-1},\, n - \rho_n) \quad (n \ge 1).
--   $$
--
--   Thus $\lambda$ is weakly increasing with $\lambda_n \le n$, so orders never cross. The value $\rho_0$ is not used, since the paper fixes $\lambda_0 = 0$.
--
--   This definition turns a realization of the random vector $\rho$ into the arrival-index path that drives the same base-stock dynamics as in the sample-path analysis; taking expectations over $\rho$ gives the expected costs studied in the paper's Section 4.2.
--
--   **Formalization Note** $n - \rho_n$ is computed in $\mathbb{N}$ and truncates at $0$. This does not change $\lambda_n$: when the paper's $n - \rho_n$ is negative, the maximum with $\lambda_{n-1} \ge 0$ ignores it either way.
-- source:
--   Janakiraman and Roundy, Lost-Sales Problems with Stochastic Lead Times: Convexity Results for Base-Stock Policies, Oper. Res. 52(5) (2004), p. 799, Assumption 2; p. 797 (lambda_0 = 0)

import Mathlib

namespace LostSalesBaseStock.Convexity

/-- Arrival index of the Kaplan lead-time model (Janakiraman–Roundy 2004, Assumption 2,
p. 799): `λ_n = max(λ_{n−1}, n − ρ_n)` for `n ≥ 1`, driven by a realization `ρ : ℕ → ℕ` of the
nonnegative integer-valued lead-time variables, with the paper's convention `λ_0 = 0` (p. 797).
`ρ 0` is never used. Natural-number subtraction truncates `n − ρ_n` at `0`; this does not change
the value, because the paper's (possibly negative) `n − ρ_n` enters only through a maximum with
`λ_{n−1} ≥ 0`. -/
def kaplanIndex (ρ : ℕ → ℕ) : ℕ → ℕ
  | 0 => 0
  | n + 1 => max (kaplanIndex ρ n) ((n + 1) - ρ (n + 1))

end LostSalesBaseStock.Convexity


